WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:16.482
Hi! We can count apples one by one, but we can't count milk. In English, whether a noun can be counted changes many little words. Today we'll learn about countable and uncountable nouns, quantifiers and articles.

2
00:00:16.632 --> 00:00:28.818
Countable nouns have singular and plural forms: one egg, three eggs. Some plurals need care: shelf becomes shelves, box becomes boxes, child becomes children.

3
00:00:28.968 --> 00:00:41.082
Uncountable nouns have no plural and don't take a: water, milk, bread, sugar, money. To count them we say a glass of milk or a loaf of bread.

4
00:00:41.232 --> 00:00:48.810
Many goes with countable nouns and much with uncountable ones. How many eggs? There isn't much milk.

5
00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:56.130
A few goes with countable nouns and a little with uncountable ones. They mean some, in a positive way.

6
00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:02.514
Without a, few and little mean hardly any. There was little water, so the plant died.

7
00:01:02.664 --> 00:01:10.602
Use some in positive sentences, and any in negatives and questions. We don't have any bread.

8
00:01:10.752 --> 00:01:21.498
A or an depends on the sound. Use an before a vowel sound: an apple, and an hour, because the h is silent. But a uniform, because it starts with a you sound.

9
00:01:21.648 --> 00:01:29.850
The is for a particular one that we both know about. I bought a book. The book is about space.

10
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.858
Watch out! This sentence has two problems!

11
00:01:34.008 --> 00:01:41.682
Friends can be counted, so we need few, not little. And being happy means having some friends, so we need a few.

12
00:01:41.832 --> 00:01:46.962
So it is: I have a few friends, so I am happy.

13
00:01:47.112 --> 00:01:57.522
Your turn! There is not, ___, milk left in the fridge. Many or much? Pause and think.

14
00:01:57.672 --> 00:02:03.474
Much, because milk is uncountable. Did you get it right?

15
00:02:03.624 --> 00:02:14.226
Another one! There was, ___, water, so the plant died. A little or little? Pause and think.

16
00:02:14.376 --> 00:02:21.546
Little. The plant died, so there was hardly any water. Did you get it right?

17
00:02:21.696 --> 00:02:31.866
Next one! How, ___, eggs do we need for the cake? Much or many? Pause and think.

18
00:02:32.016 --> 00:02:42.618
Many. We can count eggs one by one, so egg is a countable noun. For milk, we would ask how much milk. Did you get it right?

19
00:02:42.768 --> 00:02:52.482
Last one! She waited for, ___, hour. A or an? Pause and think.

20
00:02:52.632 --> 00:03:00.402
An. The h in hour is silent, so it starts with a vowel sound. Did you get it right?

21
00:03:00.552 --> 00:03:14.346
Remember: first ask if the noun can be counted. Countable nouns take many, a few and few; uncountable nouns take much, a little and little. With a it means some; without a it means hardly any.

22
00:03:14.496 --> 00:03:18.882
A or an depends on the sound, and the is for a particular one.

23
00:03:19.032 --> 00:03:24.714
Open the fridge: which foods can you count, and which can't you?

